0,8,2,8,2,8,0,8,2,8,2,8,0,8,0],[0,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,0,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,0],[0,0,0,0,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,0,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Every input has the same structure. There is a 4×4 lattice of 3×3 rings of colour 8. The rings sit at rows and columns 1–3, 5–7, 9–11 and 13–15. They are separated by background \"gutter\" lines at rows and columns 0, 4, 8, 12 and 16, so the lattice period is 4. Each ring has a hollow centre at a position where `y % 4 == 2` and `x % 4 == 2`. On top of this lattice there are a few stray marker cells. Each marker colour appears 2 or more times.\n\n**What happens to the markers.** I checked each marker colour against the output:\n\n- **Pair 1, colour 6.** The markers are at (1,4) and (4,16). The output draws a colour-6 rectangle along the gutters at rows 0..4 and columns 4..16. Every ring centre inside it becomes 6.\n- **Pair 1, colour 1.** The markers are at (13,8) and (16,0). The output rectangle covers rows 12..16 and columns 0..8, and the centres inside are filled.\n- **Pair 2, colour 2.** The markers are at (0,0) and (8,12). The rectangle covers rows 0..8 and columns 0..12.\n- **Pair 3, colour 7.** The markers are at (0,12) and (3,16). The rectangle covers rows 0..4 and columns 12..16.\n- **Pair 3, colour 3.** The markers are at (8,1) and (12,11). The rectangle covers rows 8..12 and columns 0..12.\n\nSo the rectangle comes from the bounding box of all cells of that marker colour. Each side is pushed outward to the nearest gutter line: the low edge rounds down to a multiple of 4 and the high edge rounds up to a multiple of 4.\n\nInside the rectangle only two things change:\n\n- the rectangle's outline, which lies entirely on gutter cells (all background), takes the marker colour;\n- every ring centre strictly inside the rectangle takes the marker colour.\n\nInterior gutter cells stay 0. For example, row 4 in pair 2 is unchanged. The 8-rings are never touched.\n\n**Test input.** Colour 4 has markers at (1,4) and (7,12). The rectangle is rows 0..8 and columns 4..12, and its outline plus the 4 ring centres inside become 4. Colour 2 has four markers: (13,12), (14,6), (14,10) and (15,4). Their bounding box spans y 13..15 and x 4..12. Snapping gives rows 12..16 and columns 4..12, so the outline is drawn and the two centres (14,6) and (14,10) are filled; both were already marked.\n\n## Program","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    P = 4  # lattice period (gutters every 4 cells)\n    colours = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg and c != 8]\n    for c in colours:\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        y0 = (ys.min() // P) * P\n        x0 = (xs.min() // P) * P\n        y1 = min(-(-ys.max() // P) * P, H - 1)\n        x1 = min(-(-xs.max() // P) * P, W - 1)\n        # outline along the gutters\n        out[y0, x0:x1 + 1] = c\n        out[y1, x0:x1 + 1] = c\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x0] = c\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x1] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Colour {c}: draw the gutter rectangle rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1} enclosing its markers.\")\n        # fill ring centres inside\n        for y in range(y0 + 1, y1):\n            for x in range(x0 + 1, x1):\n                if y % P == 2 and x % P == 2:\n                    out[y, x] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Colour {c}: fill the centres of every ring inside that rectangle.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"y are separated by background \"gutter\" lines at rows and columns 0, 4, 8, 12 and 16, so the lattice period is 4."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Interior gutter cells stay 0."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Colour 1: draw the gutter rectangle rows 12-16, cols 0-8 enclosing its markers."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Colour 6: draw the gutter rectangle rows 0-4, cols 4-16 enclosing its markers."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each side is pushed outward to the nearest gutter line: the low edge rounds down to a multiple of 4 and the high edge rounds up to a multiple of 4."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rectangle comes from the bounding box of all cells of that marker colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Their bounding box spans y 13..15 and x 4..12."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"There is a 4×4 lattice of 3×3 rings of colour 8."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rings sit at rows and columns 1–3, 5–7, 9–11 and 13–15."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"On top of this lattice there are a few stray marker cells."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"eda36fb962275287270e0d0b318921d030f6ec0ab74fd77312bb0bde0f8bdc50","task_sha256":"583268a6c45debb7f6ebbce763b8c83a26169b46c212a4cf5009b97ba3a00d00","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
